WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.460
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:09.200
We have previously talked about a technique
that used a deep neural network to transfer

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.849
the artistic style of a painting to an arbitrary
image, for instance, to a photograph.

00:00:14.849 --> 00:00:18.480
As always, if you're not familiar with some
of these terms, we have discussed them in

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.359
previous episodes, and links are available
in the description box, make sure to check

00:00:22.359 --> 00:00:23.560
them out!

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:28.179
Style transfer is possible on still images.
As there is currently no technique to apply

00:00:28.179 --> 00:00:33.850
this to videos, it is hopefully abundantly
clear that a lot of potential still lies dormant

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:39.410
inside. But can we apply this artistic style
transfer to videos? Would it work if we would

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:45.530
simply try? For an experienced researcher,
it is flagrantly obvious that it's an understatement

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:52.210
to say that that it wouldn't work. It would
fail in a spectacular manner, as you can see here.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:55.059
But with this technique, it apparently works
quite well.

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:59.899
To be frank, the results look gorgeous. So
how does it work?

00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:05.950
Now, don't be afraid, you'll be presented
with a concise, but deliberately obscure statement:

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:11.560
This technique preserves temporal coherence
when applying the artistic style by incorporating

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.140
the optical flow of the input video.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:19.780
Now, the only question is what temporal coherence
and optical flow means.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:24.719
Temporal coherence is a term that was used
by physicists to describe, for instance, how

00:01:24.719 --> 00:01:30.219
the behavior of a wave of light changes, or
stays the same if we observe it at different

00:01:30.219 --> 00:01:35.659
times. In computer graphics, it is also an
important term because oftentimes, we have

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:41.250
techniques that we can apply to one image,
but not necessarily to a video, because the

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:46.399
behavior of the technique changes drastically
from frame to frame, introducing a disturbing

00:01:46.399 --> 00:01:49.759
flickering effect that you can see in this
video here.

00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:54.399
We have the same if we do the artistic style
transfer, because there is no communication

00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:59.950
between the individual images of the video.
The technique has no idea that most of the

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:05.090
time we're looking at the same things, and
if so, the artistic style would have to be

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:11.390
applied the same way over and over to these
regions. We are clearly lacking temporal coherence.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:17.860
Now, onto optical flows. Imagine a flying
drone that takes a series of photographs while

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:23.440
hovering and looking around above us. To write
sophisticated navigation algorithms, the drone

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:29.260
would have to know which object is which across
many of these photographs. If we have slightly

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:34.850
turned, most of what we see is the same, and
only a small part of the new image is new

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:40.390
information. But the computer doesn't know
that, as all it sees is a bunch of pixels.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:45.150
Optical flow algorithms help us achieving
this by describing the possible motions that

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:50.420
give us photograph B from photograph A. In
this application, what this means is that

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:55.290
there is some inter-frame communication, the
algorithm will know that if I colored this

00:02:55.290 --> 00:03:01.330
person this way a moment ago, I cannot drastically
change the style of that region on a whim.

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:05.980
It is now easy to see why naively applying
such techniques to many individual frames

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:10.080
would be a flippant attempt to create beautiful,
smooth looking videos.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:15.560
So now, it hopefully makes a bit more sense:
This technique preserves temporal coherence

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:21.750
when applying the artistic style by incorporating
the optical flow of the input video.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:26.030
Such great progress in so little time. Loving it.

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:30.690
Last time I've mentioned Kram from the comments
section, and this time, I'd like to commend

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:35.730
Relatedgiraffe for his insightful comments.
Thanks for being around and I've definitely

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:40.930
learned from you Fellow Scholars! I am really
loving the respectful and quality discussions

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:45.190
that take place in the comments section, and
it is really cool that we can both learn from

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:45.930
each other.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:49.400
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

